DNS (Domain Name System)
	DNS translates human-readable domain names into IP addresses.

1. DNS Resolution Flow - 
	Browser cache -> OS Cache -> Recursive DNS resolver -> Root DNS Server -> TLD Server (.com) -> Authoritative Name server -> IP Address.
	
2. Important DNS Records - 
	| Record    | Purpose                       |
	| --------- | ----------------------------- |
	| **A**     | Domain → IPv4 address         |
	| **AAAA**  | Domain → IPv6 address         |
	| **CNAME** | Alias → another domain        |
	| **MX**    | Mail server                   |
	| **TXT**   | Text/verification information |

3. Recursive vs Authoritative DNS - 
	Recursive - finds the answer for you. Client → Recursive Resolver. It may query Root → TLD → Authoritative and then return the answer.
	Authoritative - Owns the official DNS records for a domain. example.com → IP

4. DNS Caching - 
	DNS uses caching at multiple levels - browser, operating system, Recursive resolver, Authoritative server
	
5. How DNS Achieves High Availability - 
	DNS itself needs to be highly reliable because if DNS fails, users may not even know where your server is.
	Anycast - The same DNS IP can be advertised from many locations. DNS IP -> India server or USA server.
	Multiple authoritative servers - Domains normally have multiple authoritative name servers. if one fails second handles the traffic.
	GeoDNS - DNS can return different IPs depending on where the user is located.
	
