TCP vs UDP

1. Transport layer - 
	Both TCP and UDP operate above IP. IP gets the packet to the right machine. TCP/UDP gets data to the correct program using ports.

2. TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) -
	connection oriented, Reliable, Ordered, A byte stream, not a message protocol.
	client -> TCP handshake -> Connection -> Data
	TCP provides - 3 way handshake, Reliable delivery, Ordered delivery, Retransmission, Duplicate detection, Flow control, Congestion control, Backpressure.
	TCP 3 way handshake - client -> syn to server -> syn-ack to client -> ack to server. Connection established.

3. TCP is a byte streams - 
	if the application sends message a,b,c. TCP does not preserve those message boundaries. The reciever might recieve message a+b,c or a, b+c or even partial messages. Therefore, the application protocol must define message boundaries using things like:length prefixes, Delimiters, Structured formats.
	
4. TCP Head of line Blocking - 
	suppose 4 packets are going and packet 2 gets dropped or lost. then TCP again retransmit or look for packet 2 and deliver it and until then packet 3 and 4 will not be delievered.
	TCP prefers complete, ordered data over stale or incomplete data.
	
5. Where TCP is used - 
	http/1.1, http/2, https, ssh, postgresql.
	
6. UDP (User Data Protocol) - 
	it's Connectionless, Lightweight.
	Client -> UDP -> Server.
	
7. What UDP does not Provide - 
	Retransmission, Ordering, Reliability, Flow Control, Congestion Control
	A packet may arrive, arrive late, arrive twice, arrive out of order, never arrive.
	
8. UDP preserver message boundaries - 
	if application sends - datagram a,b,c
	udp treats them different datagrams.
	
9. Why use udp?
	when Fresh data is more valuable than perfect delivery.
	e.g - online game
	
10. Where UDP Is Used
	e.g  - dns, online gaming, voice/video, QUIC. 
	use case - Real-time + low latency + can tolerate some inconsistency.
	
