Load Balancing Algorithms - 
	Load balancing distributes incoming traffic across multiple servers so that one server doesn't become the unfortunate office employee doing everyone's work.
	Main goals - Prevent server overload, improve response time, increase availability.
	
1. Round Robin - 
	Requests are distributed sequentially.
	it's very simple and even distribution of requests.
	Doesn't consider current server load , doesn't consider server performance.
	
2. Weighted Round Robin - 
	Each server gets a weight based on its capacity.
	e.g - server 1 (weigth 5) , server 2 (weight 1), server 3 (weight 1), Server 1 receives roughly 5× as much traffic as Server 2 or 3.
	it's best when servers has different cpu capacity, memory or processing power. But it still doesn't consider current load or response time.
	
3. Least Connections - 
	Send the request to the server with the fewest active connections.
	use when requests/connections have different lifetimes.
	it adapts to current load and helps prevent overloaded servers.
	It requires tracking active connections.
	
4. Least Response Time - 
	Send traffic to the server currently responding fastest.
	use when server performance varies.
	it can reduce latency, adapts to changing server performance.
	it requires accurate server response-time measurements.
	
5. IP Hash - 
	Hash the client's IP address to determine the server.
	use when you need session persistence/sticky sessions.
	it's useful for stateful applications.
	it can create uneven load, Server failures/additions can disrupt mappings (adding or removing a server), Many users behind the same NAT may appear under one public IP.
	
